// This provider-free package is a compact tour of every useful Draft 1
// judgment placement. The judgments review source and compiler-provided typed
// facts; they own no runtime state and emit no instructions. Ordinary
// assertions separately enforce the objective behavior that the program can
// check for itself. See specification section 8, "Agent judgments."
package judgment_tour

// The package judgment reviews the whole package and attaches a small design
// contract as additional evidence. A package-level claim is also available as
// guidance to every later synthesis site, although this handwritten tour has
// no synthesis and therefore needs no resolution manifest.
judge `The package follows DESIGN.md: Counter has one i64 value, counter_add is
the only procedure that mutates a Counter through a pointer, and the executable
checks the demonstrated operations with ordinary assertions.`
    file "DESIGN.md"

// Counter is a plain value record with no hidden ownership. Draft 1 does not
// have method declarations, so behavior that another language might spell as
// Counter.add is an ordinary procedure taking ^Counter below.
Counter :: struct {
    value: i64,

    // A type-member judgment reviews the enclosing type and its member/layout
    // state at this exact member position. It is not a runtime member and does
    // not change Counter's size or alignment.
    judge `Counter contains exactly one member named value of type i64 and owns
no indirect resource.`
}

// Choice supplies an exhaustive switch example without relying on an integer
// default case. Its representation is ordinary Draft enum storage.
Choice :: enum u8 {
    left,
    right,
}

// counter_add is the Draft 1 equivalent of a small mutating method. The caller
// owns the Counter and keeps its address valid for the call; this procedure
// retains no pointer and changes only the pointed-to value.
counter_add :: proc(counter: ^Counter, amount: i64) {
    // A procedure judgment sees the enclosing procedure, visible counter and
    // amount bindings, and the typed facts at this exact program point.
    judge `counter_add changes only counter^.value, adds amount exactly once,
and does not retain counter.`

    counter^.value += amount
}

// choose_with_if demonstrates judgments in distinct runtime control-flow
// regions. Each selected judgment is evaluated once by the judge command, not
// when a particular branch happens to execute.
choose_with_if :: proc(
    take_left: bool,
    left: i64,
    right: i64,
) -> i64 {
    if take_left {
        // The request includes the branch refinement take_left == true.
        judge `This branch returns left without mutating either candidate.`
        return left
    }

    // Every control-flow path reaching this point has the complementary
    // refinement. This claim therefore tests a post-branch program point.
    judge `Every path reaching this point has take_left == false, and this path
returns right without mutating either candidate.`
    return right
}

// sum_values demonstrates a judgment nested in a loop. The judge command
// evaluates the site once with fixed-point loop facts; it does not create one
// agent request per runtime iteration.
sum_values :: proc(values: []i64) -> i64 {
    total: i64
    for value in values {
        judge `The loop body adds the current value to total exactly once and
does not mutate the borrowed values slice.`
        total += value
    }
    return total
}

// choose_with_switch demonstrates that judgments in separate switch cases are
// independently selectable and receive the case's static refinement.
choose_with_switch :: proc(
    choice: Choice,
    left: i64,
    right: i64,
) -> i64 {
    switch choice {
    case .left:
        judge `The .left case returns left and does not mutate either input.`
        return left
    case .right:
        judge `The .right case returns right and does not mutate either input.`
        return right
    }
}

// Compile-time selection removes the unselected branch before semantic agent
// sites are collected. Listing this package for macOS therefore shows only the
// else judgment; listing it for Linux shows only the first judgment.
when target.os == .linux {
    judge `This package-level judgment is present only in the selected Linux
program.`
} else {
    judge `This package-level judgment is present only when the selected target
is not Linux.`
}

// main proves the executable behavior with ordinary assertions. Judgments are
// intentionally absent from this correctness path: building or running the
// program never contacts a judging provider and never consumes judgment
// evidence.
main :: proc() -> int {
    counter := Counter{value = 40}
    counter_add(&counter, 2)
    assert(counter.value == 42)

    assert(choose_with_if(true, 11, 22) == 11)
    assert(choose_with_if(false, 11, 22) == 22)

    values := [3]i64{10, 20, 12}
    assert(sum_values(values[:]) == 42)

    assert(choose_with_switch(.left, 33, 44) == 33)
    assert(choose_with_switch(.right, 33, 44) == 44)
    return 0
}
